 "drivingQuestion": "Why is the basket not a base for a prohibited object?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "the mishna is set in a basket of fruit that would be soiled if dumped",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "shabbat.142a.4",
   "daf": "142a",
   "sentenceId": 3,
   "he": "כַּלְכַּלָּה וְהָאֶבֶן בְּתוֹכָהּ. וְאַמַּאי? תֶּיהְוֵי כַּלְכָּלָה בָּסִיס לְדָבָר הָאָסוּר! אָמַר רַבָּה בַּר בַּר חָנָה אָמַר רַבִּי יוֹחָנָן: הָכָא בְּכַלְכַּלָּה מְלֵאָה פֵּירוֹת עָסְקִינַן. וְלִישְׁדִּינְהוּ לְפֵירֵי, וְנִישְׁדֵּי לְאֶבֶן, וְנִינְקְטִינְהוּ בְּיָדַיִם! כִּדְרַבִּי אִלְעַי אָמַר רַב: בְּפֵירוֹת הַמִּיטַּנְּפִין, הָכָא נָמֵי: בְּפֵירוֹת הַמִּיטַּנְּפִין.",
   "en": "We learned in the mishna: «And» it is permissible to take «a basket with a stone inside it» on Shabbat. The Gemara asks: «And why» may he do so? The «basket should be a base for a prohibited object,» and a base for a prohibited object is set-aside and may not be moved on Shabbat. «Rabba bar bar Ḥana said» that «Rabbi Yoḥanan said: Here, we are dealing with a basket full of fruit.» The basket is a base for permitted items as well, not only a base for the stone. The Gemara asks: Why may he move the basket and the stone? There is an alternative. «And let him throw the fruit and throw» the «stone» out of the basket, «and take» the fruit «in his hands,» and there will be no need to move the stone. The Gemara answers: «As Rabbi Elai» said that «Rav said» in a different context: It is referring «to fruits that become soiled» and ruined. «Here, too,» it is referring «to fruits that become soiled» and ruined if he throws them down."
  }
 ],
